/**
 * Story 5.8 (FR40): a subsetted, base64-encoded "Noto Sans" TrueType font,
 * embedded into PDF exports (`../report-export.ts`) so the Indian Rupee
 * sign (₹, U+20B9) renders correctly.
 *
 * WHY THIS EXISTS: jsPDF's built-in "standard 14" PDF fonts (Helvetica,
 * Times, Courier) use WinAnsiEncoding, a single-byte 1990s-era Windows code
 * page that predates Unicode 6.0 (2010) -- the version that actually added
 * U+20B9. Without a custom font, jsPDF/jspdf-autotable silently mis-encodes
 * every "₹" character `formatAmount()` produces into a WRONG, unrelated
 * WinAnsi glyph (confirmed via a real generated PDF rendered to a PNG during
 * this story's live verification: every Amount cell showed a stray
 * superscript "¹" instead of "₹" -- a real, visible defect, not just a
 * theoretical one). Every exported money value goes through this PDF path
 * (Boundaries: "Every exported money value goes through formatAmount()"),
 * so this is a correctness bug for literally every PDF export, not an edge
 * case.
 *
 * SOURCE: Google's official "Noto Sans" variable font
 * (https://github.com/google/fonts/blob/main/ofl/notosans/NotoSans%5Bwdth%2Cwght%5D.ttf),
 * SIL Open Font License 1.1 (full text: `./NotoSans-OFL.txt`, fetched from
 * the same source repo -- the OFL requires the license text travel with
 * the font). Pinned to its default instance (wght=400, wdth=100, i.e.
 * "Regular") via `fonttools.varLib.instancer`, then subsetted via
 * `fonttools.subset` to Basic Latin + Latin-1 Supplement + Latin
 * Extended-A/B/Additional + General Punctuation + Currency Symbols (covers
 * ₹ plus €/£/¥ for safety) + Latin Extended-C/D -- broad enough for
 * arbitrary user-entered Person/Project/Notes text (accented names, etc,
 * not just the fixed money-formatting alphabet), not narrowed to only the
 * literal characters this story's own fixtures happen to use. Regenerating
 * this file (e.g. to widen script coverage) requires `pip install
 * fonttools`; the exact subsetting command is recorded in this story's
 * Implementation Notes (spec-5-8-report-export.md).
 *
 * Dynamically imported ONLY inside `exportReportToPdf` (never at module top
 * level) -- this ~140KB asset never loads into the initial page bundle,
 * mirroring Decision #4's "dynamic import to keep heavy export-only code
 * out of the bundle" rule, extended from the 3 named libraries to this
 * font asset for the identical reason.
 */
export const NOTO_SANS_SUBSET_BASE64 =
"";
